A||||4||qwikgta||Slave or Master....how do you know which one to replace.||||1||
Z||000000||qwikgta||10-19-2001||09:04 PM||||About 6 months ago, the slave on my 88, 2.5, 5-speed went south.  I replaced it and at the same time I replaced the Master cyl too.   I replaced em, bled the system and it worked great for about 2000 miles, now I am broke **** again.  Not sure which is broke, how do you tell.  I can bleed the system and it will drive for about 50 miles and then BAM the peddle will not activate the clutch.  It bleeds easy and other than being a pain in the A** to do every 50 miles it works fine.  So again, is there a way to tell which one is broke so I don't go out and spend the cash on both if I don't need to.<P>Thanks<P>Rob||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroMaster88||10-19-2001||11:23 PM||||I dont know how to tell which is defective.  But I have a couple questions for you.  Where did you get the master and slave cylinder from and was there anything special you did to bleed it?  I just replaced both on my 88 coupe and I cant get the clutch to disengauge...just <B>WONT</B> work!  And that make me very angry <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">.<P>Thanks and good luck!<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jamessig.jpg">                  <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>Not your ordinary 88 coupe!||||reg||1
Z||000002||SLYGUY||10-20-2001||02:23 PM||||I ingnored the forum and replaced the master with a rebuilt one from Autozone. Very bad thing to do. After about four hours trying to bleed it I gave up and bought a new one from the dealer for $111. Thirty bucks more than the rebuilt but bled with a mityvac in 20 mins and works well.<BR>Just my $.02<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||10-20-2001||03:51 PM||||I's hard to say without seeing your car in person, but I suspect a bad slave cylinder.<P>What you need to do is remove the slave from it's bracket.....you don't need to remove the hydraulic line.....and remove the rubber boot and rod.  Inspect the piston area for fluid. Sometimes it is obvious....the fluid pours out once the boot is removed.  <B>ANY</B> evidence of fluid is an indication that the slave is bad.<P>If everything looks good on the slave side, then it just might be the master cylinder.  Crawl under the dash and find the rod that connects the clutch pedal to the master cylinder.  Find the rubber boot that attaches at the firewall and pop it off.....again, if any fluid dribbles out then the master is shot.<BR> <BR>I'll say it again.....if/when you do buy a new slave or master cylinder, buy a real GM part.  It'll save you many hours of bleeding and a few bottles of Tylenol too.<P>Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000004||qwikgta||10-21-2001||11:13 PM||||I got them both at the same time from Pepboys.  I was told they were new, I think the master was $85 and the slave $75 or visversa.  Anyway, they went in easy and bleed easy, no probs.  And then I drove the car for about 2K miles and now it is having probs again.  I use a vacuum pump to bleed them and it works great for doing it.  The peddle is nice and firm but after about 50 miles it goes soft and will not allow me to engage or disengage the clutch.  I guess I will make a trip to the dealer and get new GM parts.<P>Rob||||reg||1
